#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""architecture-diagram-check: the committed page must be what the model says.

WHY THIS EXISTS. The architecture page is generated from architecture/workspace.dsl.
A published diagram that is quietly wrong is worse than no diagram, because it is read
as current: a page that still names a retired component, or whose mermaid fences never
close, looks exactly like a page that is current. GitHub renders a page whose fences do
not close as ONE unterminated code block, so the diagrams do not render at all.

WHAT IT CHECKS. Eleven groups, all decided from BYTES -- no docker, no network, and no
rendering, so it runs in seconds on every push and on a laptop.  The numbers below are
the groups' OWN numbers, in the order the code applies them (the code's markers are
`# --- 1.` ... `# --- 11.`; 4 and 5 are one block, and 8b is a second block inside 8):

  1. FENCES. Every mermaid block opens and closes, and no fence is glued to other text
     -- on EITHER side.  (structurizr writes each .mmd with NO trailing newline, so a
     renderer that does `cat "$f"; echo '```'` produces "  end```" -- and the page stops
     rendering.)  Fences are counted by their WIDTH, so a page may carry an ordinary
     ```bash block, or a four-backtick block, without either being mistaken for a
     mermaid block.
  2. VIEWS. The set of views on the page is exactly the set the model declares. A view
     added to the model and not regenerated is drift; a view deleted from the model and
     still on the page is drift.
  3. ELEMENTS, PER VIEW, WITH MULTIPLICITY. The elements drawn in each view are exactly
     the elements the model puts in that view -- and each of them ONCE. A page that
     simply DROPS a declared element -- the guardian, the gate -- is drift, and so is a
     page that draws the same box twice.  An element that has NO RELATIONSHIP AT ALL is
     not drawn by the renderer: `include *` draws what is in scope, and an element in no
     relationship is in no scope (measured against the real structurizr by the sixth
     audit).  Such an element is therefore not expected on the page.
  4. NODE TEXT, PER ELEMENT. Every node is compared against THE DECLARATION OF THAT ONE
     ELEMENT -- not against the model file as a bag of words. The renderer presents an
     element as
         <div style='font-weight: bold'>NAME</div>
         <div style='font-size: 70%; ...'>[Kind] or [Kind: description]</div>
         <div style='font-size: 80%; ...'>body</div>
     and the model's declaration for an element is
         alias = kind "Name" "first string" ["second string"]
     so, measured against the committed page and the committed model:
       - the BODY div carries the element's FIRST string after its name;
       - the MARKER carries the element's SECOND string only for a CONTAINER
         (a container's description is its identity in a container view); for a person
         and for a software system the marker carries no description;
       - the marker's KIND label is the element's own kind.
     Comparing against the whole model file instead of the element's own declaration is
     the difference between "is this the model's claim about THIS box" and "is this
     sentence somewhere in the model" -- and only the first one catches a page that
     swaps two elements' bodies, or relabels a person as a container.
  5. THE MARKER'S DESCRIPTION -- the other half of the same comparison, for the second
     string.  Groups 4 and 5 are ONE code block: the node's text is read once and both
     parts of it are judged against that one element's own declaration.
  6. EDGE LABELS AND TOPOLOGY. How many edges ONE PAIR of elements gets was measured
     against the REAL structurizr (the sixth audit), and it turns on whether the
     relationship had to be PROJECTED onto the boundary: a relationship whose endpoints
     are BOTH DRAWN in the view is ONE EDGE PER RELATIONSHIP -- two relationships
     between the same pair of systems draw TWO edges, each with its own label -- while
     a SYSTEM CONTEXT view draws the system as a boundary, so a relationship to
     something INSIDE it is projected onto that boundary and the projected ones are
     MERGED per pair, so three relationships from one system to three containers of
     another draw ONE edge, labelled with the FIRST of them, which is exactly what the
     committed argon page shows.  (The earlier belief that every view merges a pair
     came from hand-made fixtures, not from the renderer.)  So the page must draw
     exactly the projected relationships: a label that is not the model's is drift, a
     declared relationship that is missing from the page is drift, a relationship the
     model does not declare is drift, an edge re-pointed at a different element is
     drift, and an edge count that does not match the view's rule is drift.
  7. THE SYSTEM BOUNDARY. In a container view the renderer nests the software system's
     own containers in a subgraph named after that system. A page that moves a software
     system INSIDE that boundary -- or a container OUTSIDE it -- draws a C4 boundary
     the model does not declare, and no per-box text check can see it.
  8. THE VIEW'S OWN TITLE. Each view's mermaid block opens with a subgraph labelled
     "<Kind> View: <the system the view is of>". A page that relabels that subgraph
     claims the diagram is a view of something else.
  8b. EVERY OTHER SUBGRAPH IN THE VIEW -- a second block inside group 8. The renderer
     draws exactly two subgraphs in a container view (the title and the system
     boundary) and one in a system context view, so any OTHER subgraph is a C4 boundary
     the model does not declare, whatever it is labelled. A view kind with no title
     rule (component, dynamic) is LEFT UNDECIDED rather than judged against a title
     this file cannot derive.
  9. THE HEADER. The page's header is exactly what architecture/render writes, read
     FROM the render, so there is one source of truth and the render cannot change it
     silently.  What the render writes depends on HOW it writes it, so the value is
     derived from the FORM: `printf '%s\n' "..."` writes the string literally whatever
     the shell is (that is why the render writes its header that way), `echo "..."`
     writes it literally too as long as it carries no backslash, and an `echo` argument
     that DOES carry a backslash has a value this file cannot know -- bash's plain echo
     writes `\t` literally, and `echo -e` writes a TAB -- so it is UNCHECKED rather than
     judged.  An argument that carries an expansion -- a `$` or a backtick -- is
     UNCHECKED for the same reason.
  10. ELEMENT NAMES, GLOBALLY. Every element name the model declares is drawn, and
     every name on the page is declared -- so a renamed element cannot leave half the
     page talking about the old name.
  11. NOTHING LEFT UNDECIDED. A line inside a mermaid block that this file has no rule
     for is reported as UNCHECKED and the run FAILS. So is a view kind with no rule
     here, a node this file cannot read, a page whose header this file cannot derive
     from architecture/render, a MODEL, a PAGE or a RENDER that cannot be read as
     UTF-8, an identifier whose scope names something the model does not declare, a
     header the render writes in a way whose value depends on the shell, a
     relationship the model declares in a form this file
     cannot read, a declaration it cannot read, and a deployment environment that
     declares a deployment node with a keyword this file has no rule for. A check that
     says "ok" about something it did not look at is not a check: a parser that DROPS
     what it does not understand turns "the page says something I cannot read" into
     "the page says nothing", and that is how a box for an element that does not exist
     sails through.

WHAT THIS CHECK DOES NOT COMPARE, STATED PLAINLY SO IT IS NOT A HIDDEN CLAIM:
  - The SECOND string of a person or of a software system. The renderer DROPS it (the
    marker carries no description for those kinds), so the page cannot show it and no
    page could satisfy a check that demanded it. A model that puts a description there
    is relying on a string nobody can read.
  - Node COLOURS. The renderer derives a style line per node from the model's styles
    block; this file does not re-derive structurizr's colour maths. The node's KIND is
    already compared, in the marker text.
  - Edge SHAPE and direction, beyond the endpoints and the label.
  - Whether the .mmd files the renderer produces still match the model. That is what
    `architecture/render --check` is for, and it needs the structurizr image.
  - The page's PROSE after the header (the renderer's own trailing line). Only the
    header is compared; the trailer is not.  A heading in that prose is prose too: a
    heading is a VIEW only when a mermaid block follows it, which is how the render
    writes its views.
  - A relationship's TECHNOLOGY -- the second quoted string on a relationship line. It
    is READ, so the relationship is not invisible (an invisible relationship let a page
    MISSING that edge pass as correct), but it is not COMPARED: the renderer draws it
    in a second, smaller div, exactly like an element's second string, and this file
    does not compare those either.
  - The RENDERED FORM of a software system instance. A deployment environment may
    instantiate a software system (`softwareSystemInstance`), and the renderer draws
    something for it -- but WHAT it draws was never measured against the real renderer,
    so such an instance is compared BY NAME ONLY. If the page draws no node with that
    name, or draws one with different text, the verdict is UNCHECKED (exit 2): this
    check cannot say the page is wrong, and it says so rather than guessing.

HOW AN IDENTIFIER IS RESOLVED. `scope.alias` names the element `alias` declared
INSIDE `scope`; a bare `alias` names the element with that alias.  The scope is part
of the identity: `github.surface` is the container `surface` INSIDE `github`, so a
model that declares `surface` somewhere else does not satisfy it.  When the scope
names nothing the model declares, or the alias is declared in two scopes, the
identifier names NOTHING this file can read -- and the relationship it appears in is
UNCHECKED (exit 2), never silently resolved to an unrelated element.

`architecture/render --check` is the byte-exact check (it re-renders and diffs) and
needs the structurizr image. This one needs nothing, so it can run everywhere.

Usage: python3 bin/architecture-diagram-check
Exit 0 = the page is what the model says. Exit 1 = it is not. Exit 2 = it could not be
decided (something could not be read, or a view kind has no rule here).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the model ---

# An element declaration: `alias = kind "Name" ["first string"] ["second string"]`.
# The match is a PREFIX match on purpose: a block declaration ends with `{`.
ELEMENT_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*=\s*(person|softwareSystem|container|component)\s+"([^"]*)"'
    r'(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?'
)
VIEW_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*(systemContext|container|component|deployment|dynamic)\s+'
    r'([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)\s+"([^"]+)"(?:\s+"([^"]+)")?\s*\{'
)
INCLUDE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*include\s+(\S+)\s*$')
RELATIONSHIP_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*([A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)\s*->\s*'
    r'([A-Za-z0-9_]+(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*)\s+"([^"]*)"'
    # The TECHNOLOGY is a second quoted string, and the renderer draws it in the edge
    # label's second div.  A relationship written with one was invisible to this file:
    # it did not match, it was not recorded, and it produced no UNCHECKED -- so a page
    # MISSING that edge passed as correct.  Structurizr's own examples use it.
    r'(?:\s+"([^"]*)")?'
    r'(?:\s*\{)?\s*$'
)
# A deployment environment and the instances it declares. A deployment view draws the
# INSTANCES an environment declares, so the elements a deployment view draws are the
# ones the model instantiates there.
DEPLOYMENT_ENV_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*deploymentEnvironment\s+"([^"]*)"\s*\{')
# A line that LOOKS like a declaration (`alias = ...`) but that this parser cannot read.
# The model is full of DSL this file has no rule for -- views, styles, tags, properties
# -- so the shape alone cannot be an error. But a line that assigns something is a
# declaration, and a declaration this file cannot read is an element it will silently
# leave out of every comparison.
# `x == y` and `x != y` are the SAME shape to this file, so they must get the same
# verdict: both are lines this parser cannot read, and both are REPORTED.  A regex
# that matched one of them and not the other silently dropped half of a class.
DECLARATION_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*[^\s=!]+\s*!?=\s*\S+')
# A `group` block in the model.  Structurizr 5.0.2's model keywords are `group`,
# `person`, `softwareSystem`, `container` and `component`; the group is the visual
# grouping the renderer draws as a subgraph.
GROUP_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*group\s+"([^"]*)"\s*\{')
# The alias charset above is deliberately wider than the one ELEMENT_RE accepts:
# a declaration this file cannot read must still be REPORTED, not dropped.  The
# charset is widened so that ANY unreadable assignment is reported rather than
# dropped -- not because structurizr accepts every alias this file can read.
# Structurizr 5.0.2's DSL REJECTS an identifier that starts with a digit, so a model
# containing one is not a model the renderer would accept; this file reports the line
# instead of dropping it, so that a hand-edited file is never silently compared as if
# it were the model the renderer read.
# The three ways a model says "this environment instantiates that element".
# `softwareSystemInstance` was NOT in this list, and the omission was invisible: a
# deployment environment that instantiates a SOFTWARE SYSTEM was read as instantiating
# nothing at all, so the page could OMIT that instance and the check said "ok" -- a
# silent pass on real drift -- while a page that DID draw it was reported as drawing an
# element the model does not declare -- a false failure on a correct page.
# What the renderer DRAWS for a software system instance was measured against the
# real renderer by the seventh audit: it is drawn AS the software system, and NOT AT
# ALL when that system is the view's subject (the subject is the boundary the view is
# drawn around).
INSTANCE_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*(containerInstance|softwareSystemInstance|instanceOf)\s+([A-Za-z0-9_.]+)\s*$'
)
# A line that LOOKS like a deployment node but uses a keyword this file cannot read.
# Structurizr 5.0.2's deployment keywords are `group`, `deploymentGroup` and
# `deploymentNode`; `infrastructureNode` is not one of them, so this pattern is not a
# keyword the renderer knows -- it exists so that a line this file cannot read is
# REPORTED instead of dropped, which would hide the subtree inside it.
UNREADABLE_NODE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*infrastructureNode\b')
# A deployment node is a NESTED box in a deployment view: the model declares it, and the
# renderer draws it as a subgraph named after it. A check that says "every subgraph must
# be the view title or the system boundary" would reject a correct deployment view.
DEPLOYMENT_NODE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*deploymentNode\s+"([^"]*)"')

# The kind label the renderer puts in a node's marker. This is the renderer's own
# vocabulary, keyed by the element's OWN kind -- not a set of words that happen to
# appear somewhere in the model file.
KIND_LABEL = {
    "person": "Person",
    "softwareSystem": "Software System",
    "container": "Container",
    "component": "Component",
}

# Which kind carries its second string (its description) inside the marker.  A
# component is rendered like a container (the seventh audit measured the renderer
# draw a component with the same marker shape).
CARRIES_MARKER_DESCRIPTION = {
    "container": True,
    "component": True,
    "person": False,
    "softwareSystem": False,
}

# The label the renderer puts on the subgraph that opens a view, per view kind. The
# label names the SYSTEM the view is of, not the view's key -- which is why two views
# of the same system carry the same label.
VIEW_LABEL_PREFIX = {
    "systemContext": "System Context View",
    "container": "Container View",
    "deployment": "Deployment View",
}


def _collapse(text: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip()


def _plain(text: str) -> str:
    """The renderer's HTML to plain text.

    ONLY the renderer's own tags go. Angle brackets that are TEXT -- a description that
    names a path template like `secrets/<env>/<name>.yaml` -- are not tags, and eating
    them turns a claim the model makes into one the model does not, which is the exact
    failure this check exists to catch.
    """
    text = text.replace("<br />", " ")
    # NOT the div tags.  The renderer's own wrapper tags are already gone -- the caller
    # splits the renderer's divs out first -- and a description that CONTAINS the
    # literal text "<div>" is TEXT, not a tag.  Stripping it turned a correct page into
    # drift (the sixth audit measured it against the real renderer).
    return text


class Element:
    """One element the model declares."""

    def __init__(self, alias: str, kind: str, name: str, first: str, second: str) -> None:
        self.alias = alias
        self.kind = kind
        self.name = name
        self.first = first          # the first string after the name: the body the renderer shows
        self.second = second        # the second string after the name: a container's description

    @property
    def label(self) -> str:
        return KIND_LABEL.get(self.kind, self.kind)

    @property
    def carries_marker_description(self) -> bool:
        return CARRIES_MARKER_DESCRIPTION.get(self.kind, False)


class Model:
    """The model: its elements, their parents, its relationships, and each view."""

    def __init__(self, text: str) -> None:
        self.text = text
        self.elements: dict[str, Element] = {}     # alias -> Element
        self.by_name: dict[str, Element] = {}      # name  -> Element
        self.alias_of: dict[str, str] = {}         # name  -> alias
        self.parent: dict[str, str] = {}           # alias -> enclosing alias
        self.children: dict[str, list[str]] = {}    # software system alias -> child aliases
        self.views: list[dict] = []
        # DECLARATION ORDER matters: when several relationships project onto one pair,
        # the renderer draws that pair ONCE, labelled with the FIRST of them.
        self.relationships: list[tuple[str, str, str]] = []
        self.unparsed_relationships: list[str] = []
        self.duplicate_names: list[str] = []
        self.unreadable_declarations: list[str] = []
        # An ALIAS declared twice names two different elements, and an identifier
        # that names the alias cannot say which one it means.
        self.ambiguous_aliases: set[str] = set()
        # The elements whose EDGES cannot be compared, because a relationship that
        # names them has an end this file cannot read.
        self.edges_left_undecided: set[str] = set()
        self.unreadable_relationships: list[str] = []
        self.deployments: dict[str, list[str]] = {}   # environment title -> element aliases
        self.soft_instances: dict[str, list[str]] = {}  # env title -> aliases whose RENDERED FORM is unverified
        self.unreadable_node_envs: set[str] = set()   # environments with a node this file cannot read
        self.deployment_nodes: dict[str, list[str]] = {}  # environment title -> node names
        self.groups: list[str] = []   # the `group` blocks the model declares
        self.deployment_opaque: list[str] = []        # environments this file cannot read
        self._parse()

    def _parse(self) -> None:
        lines = self.text.split("\n")
        stack: list[str] = []          # what each open block is (an alias, or a marker)
        in_views = False
        in_block_comment = False       # inside a /* ... */ comment
        for raw in lines:
            line = raw.rstrip()
            stripped = line.strip()

            if in_block_comment:
                # A BLOCK comment: its content is prose, not DSL.  Reading it as code
                # made a commented-out declaration look like a live one.  This check
                # comes FIRST, because the closing line of a block comment often
                # starts with "*" and would otherwise be eaten by the Javadoc branch
                # below, leaving the comment open for the rest of the file.
                if "*/" in stripped:
                    in_block_comment = False
                continue
            if stripped.startswith("/*"):
                in_block_comment = "*/" not in stripped
                continue
            if not stripped or stripped.startswith("//") or stripped.startswith("*"):
                continue
            if stripped == "}":
                if stack:
                    stack.pop()
                continue
            if stripped.startswith("views") and stripped.endswith("{"):
                in_views = True
                stack.append("#block")
                continue

            push: str | None = None
            if not in_views:
                grp = GROUP_RE.match(line)
                if grp:
                    # A `group` is a VISUAL grouping: the renderer draws it as a
                    # subgraph the page may legitimately show, and the elements inside
                    # it keep their own parents (a group is not a container).
                    self.groups.append(grp.group(1))
                dem = DEPLOYMENT_ENV_RE.match(line)
                if dem:
                    self.deployments.setdefault(dem.group(1), [])
                    push = "#deployment:" + dem.group(1)
                else:
                    dnode = DEPLOYMENT_NODE_RE.match(line)
                    if dnode:
                        env = next(
                            (s for s in reversed(stack) if s.startswith("#deployment:")), None
                        )
                        if env is not None:
                            self.deployment_nodes.setdefault(env[len("#deployment:"):], []).append(
                                dnode.group(1)
                            )
                        # NOT a `continue`: the line ends with `{`, and the generic push
                        # below must open the node's block -- skipping it would leave the
                        # closing `}` to pop the ENVIRONMENT's marker instead, and every
                        # instance after it would land in the wrong environment.
                    inst = INSTANCE_RE.match(line)
                    if inst:
                        # The instance may sit inside nested deploymentNode blocks, so
                        # the environment it belongs to is the nearest one on the stack.
                        keyword, alias = inst.groups()
                        env = next(
                            (s for s in reversed(stack) if s.startswith("#deployment:")), None
                        )
                        if env is not None:
                            title = env[len("#deployment:"):]
                            if keyword == "softwareSystemInstance":
                                # The renderer draws it, but WHAT it draws was never
                                # measured: it is tracked separately so the verdict can
                                # say "this check could not tell" instead of guessing.
                                self.soft_instances.setdefault(title, []).append(alias)
                            else:
                                self.deployments.setdefault(title, []).append(alias)
                        continue
                    unode = UNREADABLE_NODE_RE.match(line)
                    if unode:
                        # A deployment node this file cannot read.  The instances inside
                        # it are still read (they are on the stack), but the BOUNDARY the
                        # renderer draws for it is not derivable, so the comparison of
                        # that view's subgraphs is left undecided rather than guessed.
                        env = next(
                            (s for s in reversed(stack) if s.startswith("#deployment:")), None
                        )
                        if env is not None:
                            self.unreadable_node_envs.add(env[len("#deployment:"):])
                        self.unreadable_declarations.append(line.strip())
                        # NOT a `continue`: the line ends with `{`, and the generic push
                        # below must open the node's block.
                    m = ELEMENT_RE.match(line)
                    if m:
                        alias, kind, name, first, second = m.groups()
                        element = Element(alias, kind, name, first or "", second or "")
                        if alias in self.elements:
                            self.ambiguous_aliases.add(alias)
                        self.elements[alias] = element
                        if name in self.by_name:
                            self.duplicate_names.append(name)
                        self.by_name.setdefault(name, element)
                        self.alias_of.setdefault(name, alias)
                        if stack and not stack[-1].startswith("#") and stack[-1] != alias:
                            # NOT `stack[-1] == alias`: an element that re-uses an
                            # ancestor's alias would put a SELF-LOOP in the parent map,
                            # and every walk over it would never end (the seventh audit
                            # measured an unbounded allocation).  The alias is ambiguous
                            # anyway, and the ambiguity is what makes the scope
                            # unresolvable -- below.
                            self.parent[alias] = stack[-1]
                        for owner in stack:
                            if not owner.startswith("#"):
                                self.children.setdefault(owner, []).append(alias)
                        if stripped.endswith("{"):
                            push = alias
                    else:
                        rm = RELATIONSHIP_RE.match(line)
                        if rm:
                            source, target, label, technology = rm.groups()
                            from_name = self.resolve(source)
                            to_name = self.resolve(target)
                            if from_name is None or to_name is None:
                                self.unparsed_relationships.append(line.strip())
                                # The end that DID resolve names an element whose edges
                                # cannot be compared: the page may be drawing the edge
                                # this relationship declares, under a name this file
                                # cannot read.  Such an edge is UNCHECKED, never drift.
                                if from_name:
                                    self.edges_left_undecided.add(from_name)
                                if to_name:
                                    self.edges_left_undecided.add(to_name)
                            else:
                                # The TECHNOLOGY is a second quoted string on the same
                                # relationship.  It is READ so the relationship is not
                                # invisible -- that invisibility is what let a page
                                # MISSING this edge pass as correct -- but it is not
                                # COMPARED: the renderer draws it in a second, smaller
                                # div, exactly like an element's second string, and this
                                # file does not compare those either.
                                self.relationships.append((from_name, to_name, _collapse(label)))
                        elif "->" in line:
                            # A line that SHAPES like a relationship but that this file
                            # cannot read.  It must be REPORTED, not dropped: dropping
                            # it left the relationship out of every comparison, which is
                            # how a page missing a declared edge passed as correct.
                            self.unreadable_relationships.append(line.strip())
                        elif DECLARATION_RE.match(line):
                            self.unreadable_declarations.append(line.strip())
            else:
                vm = VIEW_RE.match(line)
                if vm:
                    kind, ident, title, key = vm.groups()
                    name = key if kind == "deployment" and key else title
                    self.views.append(
                        {
                            "kind": kind,
                            "ident": ident,
                            "name": name,
                            "title": title,
                            "includes": [],
                        }
                    )
                    push = "#block"
                else:
                    im = INCLUDE_RE.match(line)
                    if im and self.views:
                        self.views[-1]["includes"].append(im.group(1))
                        continue
                    if DECLARATION_RE.match(line):
                        # A declaration inside the views block is not a view, a view
                        # include, or a relationship: this file cannot read it.
                        self.unreadable_declarations.append(line.strip())

            if push is None and stripped.endswith("{"):
                push = "#block"
            if push is not None:
                stack.append(push)

    def resolve(self, ident: str) -> str | None:
        """`genesis.guardian` -> the name of the element declared as `guardian` INSIDE `genesis`.

        The scope is part of the identity.  `github.surface` names the container
        `surface` that lives inside `github`: if the model declares no `github`, or
        declares `surface` somewhere else, the identifier names NOTHING this file can
        read, and the relationship it appears in is UNCHECKED.  Dropping the scope
        prefix (the old behaviour) silently resolved such an identifier to an
        UNRELATED element, so a model the renderer would reject passed this check as
        sound -- a false NEGATIVE on the rule that says what the model says.
        """
        parts = ident.split(".")
        alias = parts[-1]
        scope_path = parts[:-1]
        if alias in self.ambiguous_aliases:
            return None
        element = self.elements.get(alias)
        if element is None:
            return None
        if not scope_path:
            return element.name
        if self.elements.get(scope_path[0]) is None:
            return None
        if any(part in self.ambiguous_aliases for part in scope_path):
            # The scope names an alias the model declares more than once, so the scope
            # cannot be resolved -- UNCHECKED, never "some other element".
            return None
        within = self._within(scope_path, alias)
        if within is None:
            return None
        if not within:
            return None
        return element.name

    def _within(self, scope_path: list[str], alias: str) -> bool | None:
        """Whether `alias` is declared inside the scope the identifier names.

        None means UNDECIDABLE: the parent map holds a cycle, so the scope cannot be
        resolved and the relationship that names it must be UNCHECKED rather than read
        as another element (the seventh audit measured an unbounded allocation here).
        """
        chain = [alias]
        seen = {alias}
        current = self.parent.get(alias)
        while current:
            if current in seen:
                return None
            seen.add(current)
            chain.append(current)
            current = self.parent.get(current)
        # The chain runs inner -> outer, and the identifier names its scope
        # OUTERMOST-first: structurizr's own spelling for a depth-2 element is
        # `sys.outer.inner` (the sixth audit measured it).
        return list(reversed(chain[1 : 1 + len(scope_path)])) == scope_path

    def nearest_visible(self, name: str, visible: set[str]) -> str | None:
        """The element the renderer draws for `name` in a view that draws `visible`.

        A view that does not draw an element draws the nearest ancestor that it DOES
        draw: a systemContext view draws the software system itself and not its
        containers, so a relationship to one of those containers is drawn against the
        system. That is the renderer's projection, and the page is compared against the
        projection -- otherwise every container relationship in the context view would
        read as drift, and a check that cries wolf is a check nobody runs.
        """
        if name in visible:
            return name
        alias = self.alias_of.get(name)
        seen: set[str] = set()
        while alias:
            if alias in seen:
                # A cycle in the parent map: the projection cannot be resolved, so the
                # edge it would carry is UNDECIDABLE (the seventh audit measured an
                # unbounded allocation here).
                return None
            seen.add(alias)
            parent_alias = self.parent.get(alias)
            if not parent_alias:
                return None
            parent = self.elements.get(parent_alias)
            if parent is None:
                return None
            if parent.name in visible:
                return parent.name
            alias = parent_alias
        return None

    def one_hop(self, seeds: set[str]) -> set[str]:
        """The seeds and the elements DIRECTLY related to them -- ONE hop.

        The renderer draws a view's scope one hop out from its subject, not the
        transitive closure: in the chain a -> b -> c, a systemContext view of `a`
        draws `a` and `b` and NOT `c` (measured against the real structurizr by the
        ninth audit).  The sixth audit measured the opposite rule -- the transitive
        closure -- from a fixture that did not separate the two.
        """
        adjacency: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
        for source, target, _ in self.relationships:
            adjacency.setdefault(source, set()).add(target)
            adjacency.setdefault(target, set()).add(source)
        out = set(seeds)
        for seed in seeds:
            out |= adjacency.get(seed, set())
        return out

    def topmost(self, name: str) -> str | None:
        """The element the renderer draws for `name` at the TOP level.

        The renderer projects an endpoint UP to the level the view draws: a container
        is drawn as the software system it is in.  The walk is over the IMMEDIATE
        parent map -- a tree, so it is bounded -- and a cycle resolves to None, which
        makes the scope undecidable rather than an allocation (the seventh audit).
        """
        alias = None
        for candidate, element in self.elements.items():
            if element.name == name:
                alias = candidate
                break
        if alias is None:
            return None
        seen: set[str] = set()
        current = alias
        while True:
            if current in seen:
                return None
            seen.add(current)
            parent = self.parent.get(current)
            if not parent:
                element = self.elements.get(current)
                return element.name if element else None
            current = parent

    def scope_names(self, owner: str, kind: str) -> set[str]:
        """The elements the renderer DRAWS in a view of this kind.

        The scope is ONE HOP from the subject and the subject's descendants, projected
        onto the LEVEL the view draws (the ninth audit's measurement):

          - a systemContext view draws the subject and the TOP-LEVEL elements -- the
            persons and the software systems.  A container is drawn as its system.
          - a container view draws the subject's CONTAINERS and the same top-level
            projection of everything else.  The subject itself is the boundary the
            view is drawn around, so it is not drawn as a node.
        """
        subject = self.elements[owner].name
        seeds = {subject} | self.descendants_of(owner)
        near = self.one_hop(seeds)
        drawn: set[str] = set()
        if kind == "systemContext":
            drawn.add(subject)
        inside = self.descendants_of(owner)
        for name in near:
            if name == subject:
                continue
            top = self.topmost(name)
            if top is None:
                # The scope cannot be resolved (a cycle in the parent map).  The
                # caller turns an undecidable scope into UNCHECKED, never a claim.
                drawn.add("?unresolved:" + name)
                continue
            if top == subject:
                if kind == "container" and name in inside:
                    drawn.add(name)
                continue
            drawn.add(top)
        if kind == "container":
            for alias, parent in self.parent.items():
                if parent != owner:
                    continue
                element = self.elements.get(alias)
                if element is not None:
                    drawn.add(element.name)
            drawn.discard(subject)
        return drawn

    def descendants_of(self, alias: str) -> set[str]:
        """The names of every element inside `alias` in the model's own hierarchy.

        ITERATIVE on purpose: the recursive version blew the interpreter's stack on a
        deeply nested model, and the traceback exited 1 -- a crash the gate reads as
        drift (the sixth audit measured it).

        It walks the IMMEDIATE parent map (`self.parent`), not `self.children`.  The
        children map records every ANCESTOR of an element as a parent, which is what
        "is this element inside that scope" needs -- but walking it is exponential:
        on a 1500-deep model the number of paths through it is 2**1499, so the walk
        never finishes.  A hang is worse than a crash: it is a missing verdict that
        never arrives.  The immediate parent map is a tree, so the walk is linear.
        """
        kids: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
        for child, parent in self.parent.items():
            kids.setdefault(parent, []).append(child)
        out: set[str] = set()
        seen: set[str] = set()
        stack = list(kids.get(alias, []))
        while stack:
            child_alias = stack.pop()
            if child_alias in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(child_alias)
            element = self.elements.get(child_alias)
            if element is None:
                continue
            out.add(element.name)
            stack.extend(kids.get(child_alias, []))
        return out

    def projected_relationships(self, visible: set[str]) -> set[tuple[str, str, str]]:
        """The relationships the renderer can draw in a view that draws `visible`."""
        projected: set[tuple[str, str, str]] = set()
        for source, target, label in self.relationships:
            near_source = self.nearest_visible(source, visible)
            near_target = self.nearest_visible(target, visible)
            if near_source is None or near_target is None:
                continue
            projected.add((near_source, near_target, label))
        return projected

    def expected_for(self, view: dict) -> tuple[dict[str, int] | None, str]:
        """The elements the model puts in this view and HOW MANY TIMES, or (None, why).

        The counts are part of the contract: a deployment view draws one instance per
        deployment node, so an instance declared in two nodes is drawn twice (the ninth
        audit's D3).  A view that draws an element twice when the model puts it there
        once is drift; a view that draws it twice when the model puts it there twice
        is not.
        """
        includes = view["includes"]
        ident = view["ident"]
        owner = ident.split(".")[-1]
        if includes and includes != ["*"]:
            names = set()
            for ident_ in includes:
                resolved = self.resolve(ident_)
                if resolved is None:
                    return None, "an include names %r, which the model does not declare" % ident_
                names.add(resolved)
            # A systemContext view ALWAYS draws its subject, even when the include list
            # names only other elements: the subject is the view (measured against the
            # real structurizr by the ninth audit).
            if view["kind"] == "systemContext" and owner in self.elements:
                names.add(self.elements[owner].name)
            return {name: 1 for name in names}, ""
        if includes != ["*"]:
            return None, "the view declares no include"
        if owner not in self.elements:
            return None, "the view names %r, which the model does not declare" % ident
        # The rule is the VIEW's kind, not the element's: `container genesis` is a view of
        # the software system's children, and `systemContext genesis` is a view of the
        # system plus everything outside it.
        # The scope is ONE HOP from the subject and its descendants, projected onto
        # the level the view draws (the ninth audit's measurement against the real
        # structurizr).  The sixth audit measured a TRANSITIVE closure; that was
        # wrong, and it made a page the renderer itself wrote look like drift.
        if view["kind"] in ("container", "systemContext"):
            drawn = self.scope_names(owner, view["kind"])
            if view["kind"] == "container":
                # NOT the components inside those children: the renderer draws a
                # component only in a component view (the seventh audit measured it).
                components = {
                    element.name
                    for element in self.elements.values()
                    if element.kind == "component"
                }
                drawn = drawn - components
            unresolved = {n for n in drawn if n.startswith("?unresolved:")}
            if unresolved:
                return None, (
                    "the scope of this view cannot be resolved: %s"
                    % " ".join(sorted(n[len("?unresolved:"):] for n in unresolved))
                )
            return {name: 1 for name in drawn}, ""
        if view["kind"] == "deployment":
            # A deployment view draws the INSTANCES its environment declares, so the
            # elements it draws are the ones the model instantiates in that environment.
            title = view.get("title") or ""
            if title not in self.deployments and title not in self.soft_instances:
                return None, "the model declares no deployment environment titled %r" % title
            counts: dict[str, int] = {}
            subject = self.elements[owner].name
            # A CONTAINER instance is drawn only when its parent is the view's subject
            # (the seventh audit measured it: a deployment view of an unrelated system
            # draws the software system instance and NOT the container instance).
            # And it is drawn ONCE PER DEPLOYMENT NODE: the same container
            # instantiated in two nodes is drawn twice (the ninth audit measured it).
            for alias in self.deployments.get(title, []):
                element = self.elements.get(alias.split(".")[-1])
                if element is None:
                    return None, (
                        "the deployment environment %r instantiates %r, which the model "
                        "does not declare" % (title, alias)
                    )
                parent_alias = self.parent.get(alias.split(".")[-1])
                parent = self.elements.get(parent_alias) if parent_alias else None
                if parent is None or parent.name != subject:
                    continue
                counts[element.name] = counts.get(element.name, 0) + 1
            # A SOFTWARE SYSTEM instance is drawn AS the software system -- and not at
            # all when that system IS the view's subject, because the subject is the
            # boundary the view is drawn around.
            for alias in self.soft_instances.get(title, []):
                element = self.elements.get(alias.split(".")[-1])
                if element is None:
                    return None, (
                        "the deployment environment %r instantiates %r, which the model "
                        "does not declare" % (title, alias)
                    )
                if element.name == subject:
                    continue
                counts[element.name] = counts.get(element.name, 0) + 1
            return counts, ""
        return None, "a %s view with include * is not a rule this check knows" % view["kind"]

    def soft_instance_names(self, view: dict) -> set[str]:
        """The names this check cannot verify the RENDERED FORM of, in this view.

        A software system instance in a deployment environment is an element the
        renderer draws, but what it draws was never measured against the real renderer:
        the only fixtures are hand-made.  So this file knows the instance is declared,
        and it can see whether a node with that name is on the page -- but it cannot
        assert that the node IS that instance, nor that its text is what the renderer
        would put there.  A difference here is therefore UNCHECKED, never a claim that
        the page is wrong.
        """
        if view["kind"] != "deployment":
            return set()
        title = view.get("title") or ""
        names = set()
        for alias in self.soft_instances.get(title, []):
            element = self.elements.get(alias.split(".")[-1])
            if element is not None:
                names.add(element.name)
        return names

    def view_label(self, view: dict) -> str | None:
        """The label the renderer puts on the subgraph that opens this view."""
        prefix = VIEW_LABEL_PREFIX.get(view["kind"])
        owner = view["ident"].split(".")[-1]
        element = self.elements.get(owner)
        if prefix is None or element is None:
            return None
        if view["kind"] == "deployment":
            title = view.get("title") or ""
            return "%s: %s - %s" % (prefix, element.name, title)
        return "%s: %s" % (prefix, element.name)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- the page ---

FENCE_OPEN = re.compile(r"^## (.+)$")
FENCE_CLOSE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*```[ \t]*$")

# The renderer's whole vocabulary inside a mermaid block, measured from the committed
# pages. The id shape is the renderer's: nodes are numbered, but the view's own
# subgraph is called `diagram`, so ids are not numeric.
ID = r"[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_]*"
NODE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*(' + ID + r')\["([^"]*)"\]\s*$')
EDGE_RE = re.compile(
    r'^\s*(' + ID + r')-\.?\s*"([^"]*)"\s*(?:\.-+>|-->|-.+->)\s*(' + ID + r')\s*$'
)
GRAPH_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:graph|flowchart)\s+[A-Za-z]{1,3}\s*$")
LINKSTYLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*linkStyle\s+\S+.*$")
SUBGRAPH_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*subgraph\s+(' + ID + r')\s*\["(.*)"\]\s*$')
STYLE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*style\s+(" + ID + r")\s+.*$")
END_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*end\s*$")

DIV_RE = re.compile(r"<div\b[^>]*>(.*?)</div>", re.S)
MARKER_RE = re.compile(r"^\[(.*)\]$", re.S)


class Node:
    """One element drawn on the page."""

    def __init__(self, ident: str, raw: str) -> None:
        self.ident = ident
        self.name = ""
        self.marker = ""       # the marker text without its brackets, e.g. "Container: ..."
        self.marker_label = ""  # the kind label, e.g. "Container"
        self.marker_description = ""  # the description inside the marker, if any
        self.body = ""
        self.readable = True
        self.reason = ""

        divs = DIV_RE.findall(raw)
        if len(divs) != 3:
            self.readable = False
            self.reason = "the node has %d divs, not the 3 the renderer writes" % len(divs)
            return
        name_div, marker_div, body_div = divs
        # HOW THE RENDERER WRITES EACH FIELD -- one table, used on BOTH sides of every
        # comparison (the ninth audit's D2 and D4):
        #   VERBATIM: the element name, the marker's description, the relationship's
        #              technology, the view's title.  The renderer preserves internal
        #              runs of spaces in these, and so must this parser.
        #   COLLAPSED: the element's body, the relationship's label.  The renderer
        #              collapses whitespace in these, and so must this parser.
        # The two sides must never be normalised differently: when they were, a page
        # the renderer itself wrote was reported as drift.
        self.name = _plain(name_div)
        marker_text = _plain(marker_div)
        mm = MARKER_RE.match(marker_text)
        if not mm:
            self.readable = False
            self.reason = "the node's marker %r is not a [Kind] marker" % marker_text
            return
        self.marker = mm.group(1)
        self.marker_label, _, description = self.marker.partition(":")
        self.marker_label = self.marker_label.strip()
        # VERBATIM, like the name: the renderer writes the description inside the
        # marker exactly as the model declares it, internal spaces included.
        self.marker_description = description.lstrip()
        self.body = _collapse(_plain(body_div))


class Page:
    """The page: per view, the nodes drawn, the edges between them, and the subgraphs.

    The parser knows the renderer's WHOLE vocabulary. A line it does not recognise is
    not dropped: it is recorded as unreadable, and an unreadable line makes the run
    UNCHECKED rather than ok. A parser that drops what it does not understand turns
    "the page says something I cannot read" into "the page says nothing".
    """

    def __init__(self, text: str) -> None:
        self.view_names: list[str] = []
        self.nodes: dict[str, dict[str, Node]] = {}   # view -> id -> Node
        self.edges: dict[str, list[tuple[str, str, str]]] = {}  # view -> (from, to, label)
        self.subgraphs: dict[str, list[dict]] = {}    # view -> {id, label, members}
        self.unreadable: list[str] = []
        self.duplicates: list[str] = []
        current: str | None = None
        in_block = False
        orphan = False
        open_subgraphs: list[dict] = []
        lines = text.split("\n")
        # A heading is a VIEW only when a mermaid block follows it.  The render writes
        # its views that way and then writes PROSE, so a heading inside that prose is
        # prose -- not a view the page is missing.  Reading it as a view made this
        # check reject a page the render itself had produced.
        mermaid_at = {i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if line.strip() == "```mermaid"}
        heading_at = [i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if FENCE_OPEN.match(line)]
        for index, line in enumerate(lines):
            fm = FENCE_OPEN.match(line)
            if fm and in_block:
                # A heading INSIDE a mermaid block is not a heading: the fence is
                # still open, so the line is part of the diagram.  It has no rule
                # here, so it is reported -- and the block is NOT ended, so the
                # lines after it are still read.
                self.unreadable.append(
                    "%s: a heading inside a mermaid block: %s" % (current, fm.group(1).strip())
                )
                continue
            if fm:
                following = [i for i in heading_at if i > index]
                end = following[0] if following else len(lines)
                if not any(index < i < end for i in mermaid_at):
                    # No mermaid block under this heading: it is the page's prose.
                    break
                current = fm.group(1).strip()
                if current not in self.nodes:
                    self.view_names.append(current)
                    self.nodes[current] = {}
                    self.edges[current] = []
                    self.subgraphs[current] = []
                open_subgraphs = []
                in_block = False
                continue
            if not in_block:
                if line.strip() == "```mermaid":
                    in_block = True
                    if current is None:
                        # A block with no view heading: there is nothing to compare it
                        # against, so it is reported and its lines are not read.
                        self.unreadable.append(
                            "a mermaid block appears before any view heading"
                        )
                        orphan = True
                continue
            if FENCE_CLOSE.match(line):
                in_block = False
                orphan = False
                if open_subgraphs:
                    self.unreadable.append(
                        "a subgraph is still open when the mermaid block closes"
                    )
                    open_subgraphs = []
                continue
            if orphan:
                continue

            stripped = line.strip()
            if not stripped:
                continue
            nm = NODE_RE.match(line)
            if nm:
                node = Node(nm.group(1), nm.group(2))
                if not node.readable:
                    self.unreadable.append(
                        "%s: node %s -- %s" % (current, nm.group(1), node.reason)
                    )
                if nm.group(1) in self.nodes[current]:
                    # Two node lines with the SAME id: mermaid draws the box twice, so
                    # the page shows the element twice. Keeping only the last one would
                    # make that invisible.
                    self.duplicates.append(
                        "%s: the page draws node %s twice" % (current, nm.group(1))
                    )
                self.nodes[current][nm.group(1)] = node
                for subgraph in open_subgraphs:
                    subgraph["members"].add(nm.group(1))
                continue
            em = EDGE_RE.match(line)
            if em:
                raw_label = em.group(2)
                first_div = DIV_RE.search(raw_label)
                if first_div:
                    # The renderer writes a relationship's DESCRIPTION in the first div
                    # and, when the relationship declares a TECHNOLOGY, the technology
                    # in a second smaller div.  Only the description is compared -- the
                    # same convention this file already uses for an element's second
                    # string.  Comparing the whole label called the renderer's own
                    # output drift.
                    raw_label = first_div.group(1)
                label = _collapse(_plain(raw_label))
                self.edges[current].append((em.group(1), em.group(3), label))
                continue
            sm = SUBGRAPH_RE.match(line)
            if sm:
                subgraph = {"id": sm.group(1), "label": sm.group(2), "members": set()}
                self.subgraphs[current].append(subgraph)
                open_subgraphs.append(subgraph)
                continue
            if END_RE.match(stripped):
                if not open_subgraphs:
                    self.unreadable.append(
                        "%s: an `end` closes a subgraph that was never opened" % current
                    )
                else:
                    open_subgraphs.pop()
                continue
            if GRAPH_RE.match(stripped) or LINKSTYLE_RE.match(stripped) or STYLE_RE.match(stripped):
                continue
            self.unreadable.append(
                "%s: a line inside the mermaid block that this check has no rule for: %s"
                % (current, stripped[:80])
            )

    def names_in(self, view: str) -> list[str]:
        """The names drawn in a view, WITH multiplicity: a box drawn twice is drift."""
        return [node.name for node in self.nodes.get(view, {}).values()]

    def subgraph_labelled(self, view: str, label: str) -> dict | None:
        for subgraph in self.subgraphs.get(view, []):
            if subgraph["label"] == label:
                return subgraph
        return None


ECHO_ESCAPES = {
    "a": "\a",
    "b": "\b",
    "e": "\x1b",
    "f": "\f",
    "n": "\n",
    "r": "\r",
    "t": "\t",
    "v": "\v",
    "\\": "\\",
}


def _read_text(path: str) -> tuple[str | None, str]:
    """Read a file as UTF-8, or say why it could not be read.

    A file that cannot be read is UNCHECKED -- never drift, and never a crash.  A byte
    that is not valid UTF-8 is something this check cannot decide, so it must not claim
    the page is wrong; and a traceback is not a verdict either.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            return handle.read(), ""
    except UnicodeDecodeError as exc:
        return None, "it is not readable as UTF-8 (%s)" % exc
    except OSError as exc:
        return None, "it could not be read (%s)" % exc


def _unescape_double_quotes(text: str) -> str:
    """What the shell does to a backslash inside a double-quoted string.

    Inside double quotes the shell keeps a backslash that is followed by anything
    other than `"`, `\`, `$`, a backtick or a newline: it is the echo builtin, not
    the shell, that turns `\\t` into a TAB.
    """
    out: list[str] = []
    index = 0
    while index < len(text):
        char = text[index]
        if char == "\\" and index + 1 < len(text):
            nxt = text[index + 1]
            if nxt in ('"', "\\", "$", "`"):
                out.append(nxt)
                index += 2
                continue
            if nxt == "\n":
                index += 2
                continue
        out.append(char)
        index += 1
    return "".join(out)


def _echo_expand(text: str) -> str:
    """What bash's echo builtin writes for `text` (the same escapes as printf %b)."""
    out: list[str] = []
    index = 0
    while index < len(text):
        char = text[index]
        if char == "\\" and index + 1 < len(text):
            nxt = text[index + 1]
            if nxt == "c":
                # `\c` stops the output: everything after it is discarded.
                return "".join(out)
            if nxt == "0":
                digits = ""
                probe = index + 2
                while probe < len(text) and len(digits) < 3 and text[probe] in "01234567":
                    digits += text[probe]
                    probe += 1
                if digits:
                    out.append(chr(int(digits, 8) & 0xFF))
                    index = probe
                    continue
            if nxt == "\n":
                index += 2
                continue
            if nxt in ECHO_ESCAPES:
                out.append(ECHO_ESCAPES[nxt])
                index += 2
                continue
        out.append(char)
        index += 1
    return "".join(out)


def _has_unescaped(text: str, char: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `text` holds a `char` the shell would expand."""
    index = 0
    while index < len(text):
        if text[index] == "\\":
            index += 2
            continue
        if text[index] == char:
            return True
        index += 1
    return False


def expected_header(render_path: str) -> tuple[list[str] | None, str]:
    """The header architecture/render writes, read FROM architecture/render.

    The header is a promise the render makes on every line of the page. Deriving the
    expected text from the render itself keeps ONE source of truth: change the render
    and this check compares against the new text, instead of against a second copy
    that can drift away from the first.
    """
    try:
        with open(render_path, encoding="utf-8") as handle:
            text = handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return None, "architecture/render is missing, so the page's header cannot be checked"
    except UnicodeDecodeError:
        return None, (
            "architecture/render is not readable as UTF-8, so the page's header cannot "
            "be checked"
        )
    block = re.search(r"render_page\(\)\s*\{(.*?)\n\}", text, re.S)
    if not block:
        return None, "architecture/render has no render_page() block"
    lines: list[str] = []
    started = False
    for raw in block.group(1).split("\n"):
        stripped = raw.strip()
        if stripped.startswith("#"):
            # A comment says what the writes below it MEAN.  It is not a write, and a
            # render is allowed to explain itself.
            continue
        if not started:
            # The block opens with blank lines -- and, in some renders, a `{` that
            # groups the writes -- before the header writes themselves.
            if not stripped or stripped == "{":
                continue
            if not (stripped.startswith("echo") or stripped.startswith("printf ")):
                return None, "architecture/render does not start the page with a header"
            started = True
        if stripped.startswith("echo "):
            argument = stripped[5:].strip()
            if not (argument.startswith('"') and argument.endswith('"')):
                return None, "architecture/render echoes something that is not a quoted string"
            raw = argument[1:-1]
            if _has_unescaped(raw, "$") or _has_unescaped(raw, "`"):
                return None, (
                    "architecture/render echoes a string with an expansion in it, so what "
                    "it writes cannot be derived from the render's own text"
                )
            # The SHELL has already resolved the double quotes: an escaped quote or
            # backtick is literal in every shell.  What is LEFT is a backslash the echo
            # builtin may or may not interpret -- bash's plain echo writes `\t`
            # literally, `echo -e` writes a TAB, and xpg_echo decides which.  This file
            # cannot know which shell will run the render, so it does not judge that
            # header at all (the sixth audit measured the opposite verdict: the page the
            # render ACTUALLY wrote was rejected).
            unescaped = _unescape_double_quotes(raw)
            if "\\" in unescaped:
                return None, (
                    "architecture/render echoes a string with a backslash in it, and what "
                    "the shell writes for that depends on the shell; the page's header "
                    "cannot be checked"
                )
            lines.append(unescaped)
        elif stripped == "echo":
            lines.append("")
        elif stripped.startswith("printf "):
            # `printf '%s\n' "..."` writes the string LITERALLY: the format does not
            # interpret escapes in the argument, and the shell has already resolved the
            # double quotes.  That is why the render writes its header this way -- the
            # value does not depend on the shell.
            rest = stripped[len("printf "):].strip()
            match = re.match(r"^'([^']*)'\s*(.*)$", rest)
            if not match:
                return None, "architecture/render writes the page with a printf form this file cannot read"
            fmt = match.group(1)
            argument = match.group(2).strip()
            if fmt == "\\n" and not argument:
                # `printf '\n'` -- a blank line.
                lines.append("")
                continue
            if fmt != "%s\\n":
                return None, "architecture/render writes the page with a printf form this file cannot read"
            if argument.startswith('"') and argument.endswith('"'):
                raw = argument[1:-1]
                if _has_unescaped(raw, "$") or _has_unescaped(raw, "`"):
                    return None, (
                        "architecture/render writes a string with an expansion in it, so "
                        "what it writes cannot be derived from the render's own text"
                    )
                lines.append(_unescape_double_quotes(raw))
            elif argument.startswith("'") and argument.endswith("'"):
                # Single quotes: the shell expands NOTHING inside them.
                lines.append(argument[1:-1])
            else:
                return None, "architecture/render writes the page with a printf form this file cannot read"
        else:
            break
    if not lines:
        return None, "architecture/render writes no header"
    return lines, ""


def main() -> int:
    root = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], capture_output=True, text=True
    ).stdout.strip() or os.getcwd()
    model_path = os.path.join(root, "architecture", "workspace.dsl")
    page_path = os.path.join(root, "docs", "explanation", "architecture", "index.md")
    render_path = os.path.join(root, "architecture", "render")

    for path in (model_path, page_path):
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            print("FAIL  architecture-diagram-check: %s is missing" % path, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    model_text, why = _read_text(model_path)
    if model_text is None:
        print(
            "UNCHECKED  architecture-diagram-check: %s -- %s" % (model_path, why),
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2
    page_text, why = _read_text(page_path)
    if page_text is None:
        print(
            "UNCHECKED  architecture-diagram-check: %s -- %s" % (page_path, why),
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2
    model = Model(model_text)
    page = Page(page_text)
    problems: list[str] = []
    undecided: list[str] = []
    # The names whose RENDERED FORM this check cannot verify, per view.  They are
    # compared by name only; any difference about them is UNCHECKED, never drift.
    soft_by_view = {
        view["name"]: model.soft_instance_names(view) for view in model.views
    }

    # --- 1. fences ---------------------------------------------------------
    raw_page = page_text
    # Fence accounting, done IN ORDER and by BACKTICK WIDTH. A page may carry plain
    # code blocks as well as mermaid blocks, and a plain block's closing fence is not a
    # mermaid block's closer: counting every bare fence against the mermaid opens
    # rejects a page for having an ordinary ```bash block in it. And a block written
    # with FOUR backticks is a legal block, closed by four backticks -- so the width is
    # part of the rule, not an afterthought.
    FENCE_RUN_RE = re.compile(r"(`{3,})")
    mermaid_opens = 0
    mermaid_closes = 0
    block_opens = 0
    block_closes = 0
    open_block = None
    glued: list[str] = []
    for line in raw_page.split("\n"):
        run_match = FENCE_RUN_RE.search(line)
        if not run_match:
            continue
        run = run_match.group(1)
        before = line[: run_match.start()].strip()
        after = line[run_match.end():]
        if open_block is None:
            block_opens += 1
            info = after.rstrip()
            if len(info.split()) > 1:
                # The renderer never writes an info string with a space in it.
                glued.append(line)
            if before:
                # A fence glued to text -- "  start```" -- is not a fence this check can
                # trust to open a block.  Recording it and then never comparing the
                # text it swallowed is how a stray "before" survived every rule.
                glued.append(line)
            open_block = {"width": len(run), "mermaid": info.strip() == "mermaid"}
            if open_block["mermaid"]:
                mermaid_opens += 1
            continue
        if len(run) >= open_block["width"] and not before and not after.strip():
            block_closes += 1
            if open_block["mermaid"]:
                mermaid_closes += 1
            open_block = None
            continue
        if before:
            # A fence glued to text -- "  end```" -- never closes the block.
            glued.append(line)
    if open_block is not None:
        problems.append(
            "%s opens a code block it never closes -- the page will not render" % page_path
        )
    if mermaid_opens != mermaid_closes:
        problems.append(
            "%s opens %d mermaid blocks and closes %d -- the page will not render"
            % (page_path, mermaid_opens, mermaid_closes)
        )
    if block_opens != block_closes:
        problems.append(
            "%s opens %d code blocks and closes %d -- the page will not render"
            % (page_path, block_opens, block_closes)
        )
    if glued:
        problems.append("a mermaid fence is not alone on its line: " + " | ".join(glued))

    # --- 2. views ----------------------------------------------------------
    model_view_names = sorted({view["name"] for view in model.views})
    page_view_names = sorted(set(page.view_names))
    if model_view_names != page_view_names:
        only_model = [v for v in model_view_names if v not in page_view_names]
        only_page = [v for v in page_view_names if v not in model_view_names]
        problems.append(
            "the page's views and the model's views differ. only in the model: %s | only on the page: %s"
            % (" ".join(only_model), " ".join(only_page))
        )

    # --- 3. elements, per view, with multiplicity ---------------------------
    for view in model.views:
        expected, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if expected is None:
            undecided.append("%s -- %s" % (view["name"], why))
            continue
        drawn = page.names_in(view["name"])
        drawn_counts: dict[str, int] = {}
        for name in drawn:
            drawn_counts[name] = drawn_counts.get(name, 0) + 1
        expected_set = set(expected)
        soft = soft_by_view.get(view["name"], set())
        missing = expected_set - set(drawn_counts)
        extra = set(drawn_counts) - expected_set
        # A software system instance the page does not draw is NOT drift this check can
        # assert: what the renderer draws for one was never measured, so the page might
        # legitimately not draw a node with that name.  So it is UNCHECKED -- a failure
        # to decide (exit 2) -- and never a claim that the page is wrong.
        soft_missing = missing & soft
        hard_missing = missing - soft
        if hard_missing or extra:
            problems.append(
                "the elements drawn in %s are not the model's: only in the model: %s | only on the page: %s"
                % (
                    view["name"],
                    " ".join(sorted(hard_missing)),
                    " ".join(sorted(extra)),
                )
            )
        if soft_missing:
            undecided.append(
                "%s draws no node for the software system instance %s the model declares "
                "in that deployment environment; this check cannot verify the rendered "
                "form of a software system instance"
                % (view["name"], " ".join(sorted(soft_missing)))
            )
        for name, count in sorted(drawn_counts.items()):
            if name in extra:
                continue
            want = expected.get(name, 0)
            if count != want:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %r %d times in %s; the model puts it there %d time(s)"
                    % (name, count, view["name"], want)
                )

    # --- 4/5. node text, against THAT element's own declaration -----------
    for view_name, nodes in page.nodes.items():
        for node in nodes.values():
            if not node.readable:
                continue
            element = model.by_name.get(node.name)
            if element is None:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %r, which the model does not declare" % node.name
                )
                continue
            # The differences about this node are collected first, because a node
            # whose RENDERED FORM this check cannot verify must report them as
            # UNCHECKED rather than as drift: "the renderer would draw it differently"
            # is not a claim this file can make.
            node_problems: list[str] = []
            if node.marker_label != element.label:
                node_problems.append(
                    "%s is drawn as [%s] but the model declares it a %s"
                    % (node.name, node.marker_label, element.label)
                )
            # The renderer COLLAPSES runs of whitespace in the model's strings, so the
            # model side must be collapsed the same way (the seventh audit measured it:
            # "two  spaces here" is written "two spaces here").
            if node.body != _collapse(element.first):
                node_problems.append(
                    "%s shows %r but the model says %r"
                    % (node.name, node.body, element.first)
                )
            if element.carries_marker_description:
                # Only a model that DECLARES a description can be missing one. A
                # container declared with a single string has no description to draw,
                # and demanding one would make every page for such a model fail --
                # a check that can never be satisfied is not a check.
                if element.second:
                    if not node.marker_description:
                        node_problems.append(
                            "%s is drawn with no description but the model declares one"
                            % node.name
                        )
                    elif node.marker_description != element.second:
                        node_problems.append(
                            "%s shows the description %r but the model says %r"
                            % (node.name, node.marker_description, element.second)
                        )
                elif node.marker_description:
                    node_problems.append(
                        "%s is drawn with the description %r, but the model declares none"
                        % (node.name, node.marker_description)
                    )
            elif node.marker_description:
                node_problems.append(
                    "%s is drawn with the description %r, which the model does not put on a %s"
                    % (node.name, node.marker_description, element.label)
                )
            if node.name in soft_by_view.get(view_name, set()):
                for text in node_problems:
                    undecided.append(
                        "%s in %s -- %s; this check cannot verify the rendered form of a "
                        "software system instance" % (node.name, view_name, text)
                    )
            else:
                problems.extend(node_problems)

    # --- 6. edges: the projected relationships, with the labels the renderer picks ---
    for view in model.views:
        visible, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if visible is None:
            continue
        view_name = view["name"]
        # The relationships the model projects into this view, IN DECLARATION ORDER. A
        # relationship whose two endpoints project onto the SAME element is internal to
        # it and the renderer draws no edge for it.
        #
        # HOW MANY EDGES ONE PAIR GETS was measured against the REAL structurizr (the
        # sixth audit), and it turns on whether the relationship had to be PROJECTED
        # onto the boundary:
        #   * a relationship whose endpoints are BOTH DRAWN in the view is ONE EDGE PER
        #     RELATIONSHIP -- two relationships between the same pair of systems draw
        #     TWO edges, each with its own label;
        #   * a SYSTEM CONTEXT view draws the system as a boundary, so a relationship to
        #     something INSIDE it is projected onto that boundary -- and the projected
        #     ones are MERGED per pair, so three relationships from one system to three
        #     containers of another draw ONE edge, labelled with the FIRST of them,
        #     which is exactly what the committed argon page shows.
        # The renderer's edge rule, SIMULATED in declaration order (measured by the
        # seventh audit against the real structurizr and validated against every view
        # of both repositories): project each end with `nearest_visible`; skip the
        # relationship when both ends land on the SAME element; skip a PROJECTED
        # relationship whose pair has already been emitted; otherwise emit the pair and
        # its label.  The earlier two-bucket guess -- "projected ones merge, direct ones
        # do not" -- was not a measurement, and it demanded one edge too many.
        model_labels: dict[tuple[str, str], list[str]] = {}
        emitted: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()
        for source, target, label in model.relationships:
            near_source = model.nearest_visible(source, visible)
            near_target = model.nearest_visible(target, visible)
            if near_source is None or near_target is None or near_source == near_target:
                continue
            pair = (near_source, near_target)
            projected = near_source != source or near_target != target
            if projected and pair in emitted:
                continue
            emitted.add(pair)
            model_labels.setdefault(pair, []).append(label)
        drawn_pairs: dict[tuple[str, str], list[str]] = {}
        for source_id, target_id, label in page.edges.get(view_name, []):
            source = page.nodes.get(view_name, {}).get(source_id)
            target = page.nodes.get(view_name, {}).get(target_id)
            if source is None or target is None:
                problems.append(
                    "an edge in %s refers to a node that is not drawn there (%s -> %s)"
                    % (view_name, source_id, target_id)
                )
                continue
            pair = (source.name, target.name)
            drawn_pairs.setdefault(pair, []).append(label)
        for pair in sorted(set(model_labels) - set(drawn_pairs)):
            if pair[0] in model.edges_left_undecided or pair[1] in model.edges_left_undecided:
                # The edge this view is missing may be the one this file cannot read.
                undecided.append(
                    "%s does not draw %s -> %s, and the model declares a relationship "
                    "this check cannot read that may be that edge"
                    % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
                )
                continue
            problems.append(
                "the model declares a relationship the page does not draw in %s: %s -> %s"
                % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
            )
        for pair in sorted(set(drawn_pairs) - set(model_labels)):
            if pair[0] in model.edges_left_undecided or pair[1] in model.edges_left_undecided:
                # A relationship this file CANNOT READ may be exactly this edge: the
                # model declares it, but one of its ends names something this file
                # cannot resolve.  The edge is therefore UNCHECKED -- this check
                # cannot say the page draws a relationship the model does not.
                undecided.append(
                    "%s draws %s -> %s, and the model declares a relationship this "
                    "check cannot read that may be that edge" % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
                )
                continue
            problems.append(
                "the page draws a relationship the model does not declare in %s: %s -> %s"
                % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
            )
        for pair in sorted(set(drawn_pairs) & set(model_labels)):
            labels = drawn_pairs[pair]
            want = model_labels[pair]
            if len(labels) != len(want):
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %d edges for %s -> %s in %s; the model declares %d "
                    "relationship(s) for that pair"
                    % (len(labels), pair[0], pair[1], view_name, len(want))
                )
            elif pair[0] in model.edges_left_undecided or pair[1] in model.edges_left_undecided:
                # The renderer draws the FIRST relationship that projects onto a pair,
                # so the label on this pair may come from the relationship this file
                # cannot read.  The label is therefore UNCHECKED, not wrong.
                undecided.append(
                    "%s draws %s -> %s with a label this check cannot confirm, because "
                    "the model declares a relationship this check cannot read that may "
                    "project onto that pair" % (view_name, pair[0], pair[1])
                )
            elif sorted(labels) != sorted(want):
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %s -> %s %r, but the model declares %r"
                    % (pair[0], pair[1], labels, want)
                )
    for name in sorted(set(model.duplicate_names)):
        undecided.append(
            "the model declares %r more than once; this check cannot tell those "
            "elements apart, so it cannot say which one the page draws" % name
        )
    for line in model.unreadable_declarations:
        undecided.append(
            "a declaration the model makes that this check cannot read: %s" % line
        )
    for line in model.unparsed_relationships:
        undecided.append("a relationship the model declares that this check cannot resolve: %s" % line)
    for line in model.unreadable_relationships:
        undecided.append(
            "a relationship the model declares that this check cannot read: %s" % line
        )

    # --- 7. the system boundary, per container view -------------------------
    for view in model.views:
        if view["kind"] != "container":
            continue
        expected, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if expected is None:
            continue
        owner_alias = view["ident"].split(".")[-1]
        owner = model.elements.get(owner_alias)
        if owner is None:
            continue
        view_name = view["name"]
        boundaries = [
            s for s in page.subgraphs.get(view_name, []) if s["label"] == owner.name
        ]
        if not boundaries:
            problems.append(
                "the page does not draw the boundary of %s in %s" % (owner.name, view_name)
            )
            continue
        if len(boundaries) > 1:
            problems.append(
                "the page draws %d boundaries labelled %s in %s; the model declares one"
                % (len(boundaries), owner.name, view_name)
            )
        nodes = page.nodes.get(view_name, {})
        inside = {nodes[i].name for s in boundaries for i in s["members"] if i in nodes}
        descendants = model.descendants_of(owner_alias)
        for name in sorted(expected):
            if name == owner.name:
                continue
            drawn_inside = name in inside
            should_be_inside = name in descendants
            if drawn_inside != should_be_inside:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws %s %s the boundary of %s in %s, but the model says %s"
                    % (
                        name,
                        "inside" if drawn_inside else "outside",
                        owner.name,
                        view_name,
                        "inside" if should_be_inside else "outside",
                    )
                )

    # --- 8. the view's own title ------------------------------------------
    for view in model.views:
        expected_label = model.view_label(view)
        if expected_label is None:
            undecided.append(
                "%s -- this check has no rule for the title of a %s view"
                % (view["name"], view["kind"])
            )
            continue
        labels = [s["label"] for s in page.subgraphs.get(view["name"], [])]
        if not labels or labels[0] != expected_label:
            problems.append(
                "the page draws %s as %r, but the renderer titles that view %r"
                % (view["name"], labels[0] if labels else "", expected_label)
            )
        else:
            prefix = expected_label.split(":")[0] + ":"
            for other in labels[1:]:
                if other.startswith(prefix):
                    problems.append(
                        "the page draws a second view title %r in %s; the renderer titles "
                        "that view %r" % (other, view["name"], expected_label)
                    )

    # --- 8b. every OTHER subgraph in the view -------------------------------
    # The renderer draws exactly two subgraphs in a container view -- the view's own
    # title and the system boundary -- and exactly one in a system context view.  Any
    # OTHER subgraph is a C4 boundary the model does not declare, whatever it is
    # labelled: a decoy named after another system, an extra grouping, a nested level
    # of nesting.  Comparing only the owner's own label let all of those through.
    for view in model.views:
        view_name = view["name"]
        owner_alias = view["ident"].split(".")[-1]
        owner = model.elements.get(owner_alias)
        declared = set()
        title = model.view_label(view)
        if title is None:
            # A view kind this file has no title rule for (component, dynamic).  The
            # renderer still draws its own title subgraph, and comparing it against a
            # title this file cannot derive produced a WRONG FAIL that masked the
            # honest UNCHECKED verdict.
            continue
        if view["kind"] == "deployment" and (view.get("title") or "") in model.unreadable_node_envs:
            # This environment declares a deployment node with a keyword this file has
            # no rule for, so the boundary the renderer draws for it is NOT derivable.
            # Comparing the page's subgraphs against a set that cannot be complete would
            # report a correct page as drifted, so the comparison is left undecided.
            undecided.append(
                "%s draws a deployment environment that declares a deployment node this "
                "check cannot read, so this check cannot say which boundaries the page "
                "should draw" % view_name
            )
            continue
        declared.add(title)
        if owner is not None and view["kind"] == "container":
            declared.add(owner.name)
        if view["kind"] == "deployment":
            # A deployment view nests the environment's own NODES as subgraphs.
            declared.update(model.deployment_nodes.get(view.get("title") or "", []))
        # A `group` in the model is drawn as a subgraph the page may legitimately show.
        declared.update(model.groups)
        for s in page.subgraphs.get(view_name, []):
            if s["label"] not in declared:
                problems.append(
                    "the page draws a subgraph %r in %s; the model declares no such boundary"
                    % (s["label"], view_name)
                )

    # --- 9. the header the render writes ------------------------------------
    header, why = expected_header(render_path)
    if header is None:
        undecided.append(why)
    else:
        page_lines = raw_page.split("\n")
        actual = page_lines[: len(header)]
        if actual != header:
            problems.append(
                "the page's header is not what architecture/render writes: %r" % (actual[:2],)
            )

    # --- 10. element names, globally ----------------------------------------
    # The names the page must carry are the ones the MODEL'S OWN VIEWS put in scope --
    # not every element the model declares.  The renderer draws what a view has in
    # scope: an element with no relationship is in no scope and is drawn in no view
    # (measured against the real structurizr by the sixth audit), and a model may
    # declare elements that only a view the page does not carry would draw.  Comparing
    # against the whole model instead would call the renderer's own page wrong.
    expected_names: set[str] = set()
    scope_unknown = False
    for view in model.views:
        in_scope, why = model.expected_for(view)
        if in_scope is None:
            # This view's scope is UNDECIDED.  The global comparison must then be
            # UNDECIDED too -- a hard claim about material this file cannot read must
            # never outrank an honest UNCHECKED (the seventh audit measured it
            # asserting an element set that contradicted the page).
            scope_unknown = True
            continue
        expected_names |= set(in_scope)
    model_names = expected_names
    drawn_names = {name for view in page.nodes for name in page.names_in(view)}
    if scope_unknown:
        undecided.append(
            "the scope of at least one view this model declares is not something this "
            "check can decide, so it cannot say which elements the page must carry"
        )
    elif model_names != drawn_names:
        # A name that is only ever a software system instance is the same case as the
        # per-view comparison above: this check cannot verify the rendered form of one,
        # so it cannot claim the page omits it.
        all_soft: set[str] = set()
        for names in soft_by_view.values():
            all_soft |= names
        missing_names = model_names - drawn_names
        extra_names = drawn_names - model_names
        soft_missing = missing_names & all_soft
        hard_missing = missing_names - soft_missing
        if hard_missing or extra_names:
            problems.append(
                "the page's elements and the model's elements differ. only in the model: %s | only on the page: %s"
                % (
                    " ".join(sorted(hard_missing)),
                    " ".join(sorted(extra_names)),
                )
            )
        if soft_missing:
            undecided.append(
                "the page draws nothing for %s, which the model declares as a software "
                "system instance; this check cannot verify the rendered form of a "
                "software system instance"
                % " ".join(sorted(soft_missing))
            )

    # --- 11. nothing left undecided -----------------------------------------
    for note in page.duplicates:
        problems.append(note)
    for note in page.unreadable:
        undecided.append(note)

    if undecided:
        for note in undecided:
            print(
                "UNCHECKED  architecture-diagram-check: %s -- this check has no rule that "
                "decides it, so it cannot say the page is what the model says"
                % note,
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
    if problems:
        for problem in problems:
            print("FAIL  architecture-diagram-check: %s" % problem, file=sys.stderr)
        print(
            "  run 'architecture/render' to regenerate the page from architecture/workspace.dsl",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    if undecided:
        return 2
    print("ok    architecture-diagram-check: %s is what %s says" % (page_path, model_path))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
